  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the estates, powers and trusts law, in relation to certain formula clauses to be construed to refer to the federal estate and generation-skipping transfer tax laws applicable to estates of decedents dying after December 31, 2009 and before January 1, 2011",
    "basePrintNo": "S8056",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8056-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Renum §§2-1.13 & 2-1.14 to be §§2-1.14 & 2-1.15, add §2-1.13, EPT L",
    "lawSection": "Estates, Powers and Trusts Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S8056\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the estates, powers and trusts law, in relation to\ncertain formula clauses to be construed to refer to the federal\nestate and\ngeneration-skipping transfer tax laws applicable to estates of decedents\ndying after December 31, 2009 and before January 1, 2011\n\nPURPOSE OF BILL:\nUnder the Internal Revenue Code now in effect, the Federal estate tax\nis repealed for decedents dying in 2010, only to be restored in 2011\nat higher rates and with lower exemptions. Most estate attorneys\nexpected Congress to enact a new law prior to 2010 so as to prevent\nthe estate tax from expiring and did not anticipate that temporary\nrepeal would occur. There is still a possibility that Congress may\nreinstate an estate tax for 2010 retroactively.\n\nMany wills, trusts and beneficiary forms prepared prior to 2010 were\nbased on formula dispositions in the case of married testators.\nThe aim was typically to leave as much as possible on the first\nspouse's death to a trust or to individuals other than the surviving\nspouse (typically issue of the decedent) without causing estate tax\non the first spouse's death. This was commonly referred to as a\n\"credit shelter bequest\", since the amount involved ($3.5 million for\ndecedent dying in 2009) would be sheltered from estate tax on the\nfirst death by a \"unified credit\" allowed under the Internal Revenue\nCode. The credit shelter bequest is not subject to estate tax at the\nsurviving spouse's death.\n\nThe credit shelter bequest is usually phrased in terms of the maximum\namount that can pass free of Federal estate tax on the first death,\nor in terms of like effect. The same results were obtained with\nformulas leaving a spouse outright or in a form qualifying for a\nmarital deduction against the Federal estate tax, the minimum amount\nnecessary to avoid imposition of an estate tax on the first spouse's\ndeath. With repeal of the estate tax, these formula clauses could be\ninterpreted to leave the entire estate in a credit shelter\ndisposition and nothing to the spouse or in a form qualifying for a\nmarital deduction. This conclusion is reached since the entire\nestate and not just the part formerly sheltered by statutory credits\ncan now pass under a credit shelter disposition without there being\nany estate tax.\n\nThe Federal generation-skipping transfer tax is also temporarily\nrepealed for transfers in 2010. Many wills and testamentary\ninstruments also left to grandchildren (or to trusts that would\neventually pass to grandchildren) maximum amounts allowable without\nimposition of a Federal generation skipping transfer tax. With\n\nrepeal, this amount is now unlimited, and may pass to grandchildren\nto the detriment of the decedent's children.\n\nThe purposes of the bill, accordingly, are:\n\n1. To preserve the form in which most testators expected their estates\nto be divided prior to the unexpected repeal of the Federal estate tax;\n\n2. To assure that a surviving spouse will not be disinherited and left\nto exercise a right of election under Article 5 of the Estates,\nPowers and Trusts Law;\n\n3. To shelter assets in a credit shelter disposition for the time\nestate tax may be reinstated;\n\n4. To prevent unwarranted amounts from\npassing to grandchildren to the detriment of children.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nThe bill, which applies to assets that are distributed upon the death\nof a decedent through a will\nor revocable trust, amends the Estates, Powers and Trusts Law to\nprovide that a formula in a will, or trust executed or created prior\nto January 1, 2010 providing, in sum and substance, for a bequest of\nan amount of property that can be sheltered from Federal estate tax\nby reason of\ncredits against such tax, shall be construed under the Internal\nRevenue Code of 1986 as in effect\nfor decedents dying on December 31, 2009, in the case of a decedent\nwho is survived by a spouse, and who dies during 2010 at a time when\nthe Federal estate tax is repealed. The Federal estate tax is not\ndeemed repealed if it is legally reinstated retroactively to the time\nof the decedent's death. This legislation does not apply to\nirrevocable trusts.\n\nSimilarly, if a will or revocable trust executed or created prior to\nJanuary 1, 2010 contains a bequest which in sum and substance refers\nto the decedent's unused exemption from the federal generation\nskipping transfer tax, then such bequest shall, be construed under\nthe Internal Revenue Code of 1986 in effect on December 31, 2009 with\nrespect to generation skipping transfers on such date. This applies\nin the case of a decedent who dies during 2010 at a time when the\nfederal generation skipping transfer tax is not in effect. Again, the\nfederal generation skipping transfer tax is considered to be in\neffect if legally reinstated retroactively to the date of decedent's\ndeath.\n\nThis legislation also includes a provision exempting any will or trust\nthat already has provisions drafted by the testator determining what\nshould happen with formula clause provisions in the event that estate\nor generation-skipping transfer tax is repealed.\n\nFinally, this legislation provides that any affected beneficiary or\nthe executor or trustee may bring a proceeding within a year of the\ndecedent's date of death to determine whether the decedent intended\nthat that the formula clause be construed with respect to the law as\nit existed on the decedent's date of death (rather than on December\n31, 2009). In that regard, the legislation also provides that\nextrinsic evidence may be admitted to establish the decedent's intent.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\nExisting Law following repeal of the Federal estate tax and generation\nskipping transfer tax is unclear. It is conceivable that all of a\ndecedent's property may be held by a court to pass under a credit\nshelter disposition to the detriment of a surviving spouse.\n\nIt is also possible that a decedent's children may be deprived of\nproperty all of which is held to pass under a bequest of a decedent's\nunused exemption from the Federal generation skipping transfer tax.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nWithout this legislation, due to the repeal of federal estate and\ngeneration skipping tax, when there is a formula clause in a will,\nthe intent of the testator cannot be carried out or determined\nthrough a construction proceeding. The only remedy available is a\ncostly right of election proceeding. This legislation will therefore\navoid needless litigation to the benefit of beneficiaries of the\ndecedent and the court system as well.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNew bill, 2010.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\nThere are no fiscal implications.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately, and shall be deemed to have\nbeen in full force and effect on and after January 1, 2010.\n\n",
